@prefix tmk:     <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/> .
@prefix tmkc:    <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/concept/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/legal-concepts>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "TM-Knowledge — legal concepts module"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """The concept layer.

Every concept in `eval/gold/concepts.yaml` and `authored/concepts.yaml` becomes a
`tmk:LegalConcept`. Which of the eleven kinds below it is in — what it *is* — depends
on a concept-types record and on nothing else: one a named expert signed in
`eval/gold/`, or one a machine wrote in `authored/`, unreviewed, in the authored
graph (ADR-0079, ADR-0080).

What a concept *does* is not a kind. On the owner's ruling B1 of 2026-10-09
(ADR-0126) — "the ontology should describe what a thing 'is' (as a node) and what
it 'does' as an edge" — relevant factor, exception and remedy stopped being groups
a concept is filed in and became what an edge says: a factor `qualifies` the test
it bears on, an exception `doesNotGiveRiseTo` the ground it takes a case out of, a
ground `isOvercomeBy` its remedy. `tmk:RelevantFactor`, `tmk:Exception` and
`tmk:Remedy` remain, defined by those edges, so a concept is one because of what
it does to a named thing and never because a dropdown said so.

Until a concept has a type record it stays a bare `tmk:LegalConcept`, and the count
of unsorted concepts is reported by every build rather than defaulted away."""@en-AU ;
    owl:imports <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/authority> ;
    owl:versionInfo "draft — not approved" .

tmk:LegalConcept a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Legal concept"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A concept in the examination vocabulary, with a preferred label, its variants, and — crucially — the labels it must not be confused with."@en-AU .

# --- the top level: three families, and how they connect (ADR-0126, ADR-0131) ----
#
# Every kind sits in one of three families, and the families are classes, not only
# boxes on a page: the law's questions, what they are asked about, and the process
# that asks them. How they connect is not left to the reader. Each predicate in the
# relation dictionary names the kinds it joins (`tmk:expectedSubjectKind`,
# `tmk:expectedObjectKind` in relations.ttl), so the links between families are the
# dictionary's own — and a machine-written edge whose ends are not kinds its predicate
# joins is a defect (`predicates.off_schema`, the harness). The owner's instruction of
# 2026-10-09: "Not having elements connect seems like a bit of a cop out."

tmk:LegalQuestion a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:label "The law's questions"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Grounds of refusal, the tests that decide them, and the principles that govern how they are answered.

How the family connects. A ground requires its tests (requiresElement) and a test
states the threshold for a ground (statesThresholdFor). What is examined bears on
them: sign content may give rise to a ground or test (mayGiveRiseTo), or does not by
itself (doesNotGiveRiseTo); context and sign content qualify a test (qualifies); a test
or ground applies to subject matter (appliesTo). The process answers them: a ground is
assessed in a step (isAssessedIn), a ground made out prevents acceptance (prevents), a
ground is overcome by a step, a record or a use (isOvercomeBy), and a principle
constrains a role (constrainsRole)."""@en-AU .

tmk:ExaminedMatter a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:label "What the law's questions are asked about"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """The mark and its kinds (subject matter), what it contains or conveys (sign content), the world outside it (context) and what people do with it in trade (use in trade).

How the family connects. To the law's questions, as their causes, factors and
exceptions (above). To the process: a step operates on a mark or registration
(operatesOn), a role owns one (owns), a registration is recorded in a register
(isRecordedIn), evidence establishes a use (establishes), a date is fixed by an event
and a right runs from a date (isFixedBy, runsFrom, keepsEarlierDate), and sign content
indicates what the mark conveys (indicates)."""@en-AU .

tmk:ProcessElement a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:label "The process"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Who acts (process role), what is done (procedural step), what it produces or receives (instrument or record), and the bodies of rules outside the Act it runs under (external instrument).

How the family connects inside itself. A role performs a step (performs), files a
document (files) and issues a record (issues); a step is commenced by a document
(isCommencedBy), comes before another (precedes), results in a record or a status
(resultsIn) and operates on a record (operatesOn); a record is recorded in a register
(isRecordedIn); an external instrument applies to what it governs (appliesTo). Who
holds power over whom is the authority vocabulary (authority.ttl)."""@en-AU .

tmk:GroundOfRefusal a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:LegalQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Ground of refusal"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else — never from a label or a heuristic. In the approved graph that record is one a named expert signed; in the authored graph it is one a machine wrote and nobody has read, and the two never share a graph (ADR-0080). A statutory basis on which registration may be refused."@en-AU .

tmk:LegalTest a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:LegalQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Legal test"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else — never from a label or a heuristic. In the approved graph that record is one a named expert signed; in the authored graph it is one a machine wrote and nobody has read, and the two never share a graph (ADR-0080). A question the decision maker must answer, with a stated threshold."@en-AU .

tmk:Principle a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:LegalQuestion ;
    rdfs:label "Principle"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. A principle or settled practice about how the law's questions are answered: where the onus lies, what standard of satisfaction applies, whether the decision maker has a discretion, how the office applies the Act where it leaves room — the presumption of registrability (s 33), the mandatory application of section 43, office practice.

Added on the owner's instruction of 2026-10-09 (ADR-0131). Until then these sat in
`none_of_these`, which said only that no kind fitted them; they are neither a ground
nor a test, but they govern both, and they connect to them: a principle constrains a
role, applies to a step and qualifies a ground."""@en-AU .

# --- what a concept does: defined by its edges, never asserted (B1) ----------

# The three definitions are named nodes, not blank ones: the TBox is serialised into
# the committed graph, and a blank node gets a fresh id on every build, which the
# drift check reads as a change.

tmk:QualifiesAConcept a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty tmk:qualifies ;
    owl:someValuesFrom tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "Whatever qualifies some concept — the definition of tmk:RelevantFactor."@en-AU .

tmk:GivesRiseToNoConceptByItself a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty tmk:doesNotGiveRiseTo ;
    owl:someValuesFrom tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "Whatever does not by itself give rise to some concept — the definition of tmk:Exception."@en-AU .

tmk:overcomes a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    owl:inverseOf tmk:isOvercomeBy ;
    rdfs:label "overcomes"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The inverse of tmk:isOvercomeBy, declared only so that tmk:Remedy can be defined. No record uses it: edges are written ground → remedy, as the relation dictionary says."@en-AU .

tmk:OvercomesAConcept a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty tmk:overcomes ;
    owl:someValuesFrom tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment "Whatever some ground is overcome by — the definition of tmk:Remedy."@en-AU .

tmk:RelevantFactor a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept ;
    owl:equivalentClass tmk:QualifiesAConcept ;
    rdfs:label "Relevant factor"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Something the decision maker weighs, which does not on its own decide the question. A function, not a kind (ruling B1, ADR-0126): a concept is a relevant factor because an edge says it qualifies a named test or ground, and the build never asserts this class from a typing record."@en-AU .

tmk:Exception a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept ;
    owl:equivalentClass tmk:GivesRiseToNoConceptByItself ;
    rdfs:label "Exception"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A case a ground or test does not reach. A function, not a kind (ruling B1, ADR-0126): a concept is an exception because an edge says it does not give rise to a named ground or test, and the build never asserts this class from a typing record."@en-AU .

tmk:Remedy a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept ;
    owl:equivalentClass tmk:OvercomesAConcept ;
    rdfs:label "Remedy"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A step, record or consent that overcomes a ground for rejection already made out — a condition of registration, an endorsement, a limitation of the specification, a person's written permission. A function, not a kind (ruling B1, ADR-0126): a concept is a remedy because a ground is overcome by it, on an edge naming that ground.

Split from tmk:Exception on the owner's ruling B5 of 2026-10-08 (ADR-0124): an
exception is a case the ground does not reach, so nothing needs overcoming; a remedy
answers a ground that does apply, and the applicant has to supply it."""@en-AU .

# --- what the law's questions are asked about (B1) ------------------------------

tmk:SignContent a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:ExaminedMatter ;
    rdfs:label "Sign content"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. Something a trade mark contains or conveys: a word, a name, an image, a sign within it, or a meaning — a geographical reference, an INN stem, a phoneword, a prescribed sign, an implied endorsement. Written for B1 (ADR-0126): most of what the old relevant-factor group held is this, and what it does to a ground or test is on its edges."@en-AU .

tmk:Context a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:ExaminedMatter ;
    rdfs:label "Context"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. Something outside the trade mark that the assessment looks at: the goods, the market and its ordinary consumers, a person or place the mark refers to, a reputation, a date. Written for B1 (ADR-0126)."@en-AU .

tmk:UseInTrade a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:ExaminedMatter ;
    rdfs:label "Use in trade"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. Something a person does with a trade mark in the course of trade: using it, authorising its use, using it before another or alongside another. Written for B1 (ADR-0126); what such use does — overcoming a section 44 ground, establishing capacity to distinguish — is on its edges."@en-AU .

# --- the process ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Who acts, what is acted on in the office, what act is performed, what it produces,
# and what external scheme it runs under. Added on 2026-09-09 because 53 of the
# vocabulary's 130 concepts fitted none of the owner's first four groups, and the 53
# were not a random remainder (ADR-0098). Same footing as every kind: subclasses of
# tmk:LegalConcept, filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else.
# tmk:SubjectMatter, declared below with them, now sits in the "examined" family in
# the explorer beside sign content, context and use in trade (ADR-0126).

tmk:ProcessRole a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:ProcessElement ;
    rdfs:label "Process role"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. A person or body that acts under the Act — the concept of the office, as the vocabulary holds it.

NOT tmk:Role, and the distinction is load-bearing. A tmk:Role in the examination
module is somebody who acts: an examiner who signs a report. A tmk:ProcessRole
is the *term* "examiner" as an entry in the vocabulary. Merging them would make
the concept record for examiner an examiner, and the model would lose its only
way of saying which of the two it is talking about."""@en-AU .

tmk:SubjectMatter a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:ExaminedMatter ;
    rdfs:label "Subject matter"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. The thing the process operates on — what may be registered, and the kinds of sign and registration the Act holds apart. A sign, a shape trade mark, a certification trade mark and a protected international trade mark are all subject matter; what refuses, tests or excepts them is not."@en-AU .

tmk:ProceduralStep a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:ProcessElement ;
    rdfs:label "Procedural step"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. An act, proceeding or event that moves an application or a registration from one state to the next.

Deliberately wider than "something somebody does": lapsing is in this class and
nobody performs it. What the class holds is a transition, whether it is
performed, ordered or merely suffered — which is why the modality of each step
is a property of the concept and not of the group."""@en-AU .

tmk:InstrumentOrRecord a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:ProcessElement ;
    rdfs:label "Instrument or record"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. A document, entry or endorsement the process produces or receives, or writes to the Register. The Register itself is here, as are the things entered on it — a disclaimer, an endorsement, conditions or limitations — and the documents that start or end a proceeding."@en-AU .

tmk:ExternalInstrument a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalConcept , tmk:ProcessElement ;
    rdfs:label "External instrument"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Filled from a concept-types.yaml record and from nothing else. A treaty or international scheme Australian practice adopts, rather than a rule the Act itself makes.

Small, and it earns its place on the honesty argument rather than the size one:
CLAUDE.md rule 5 turns on keeping the source of an obligation visible, and a
retrieval surface that cannot tell the Madrid Protocol from a section of the Act
has lost exactly the distinction the rule protects."""@en-AU .

tmk:expectedSubjectKind a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "expected subject kind"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A kind a predicate's subject may be (relations.ttl). An annotation, so it infers nothing; the harness checks machine-written edges against it (ADR-0131)."@en-AU .

tmk:expectedObjectKind a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "expected object kind"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "As tmk:expectedSubjectKind, for the object."@en-AU .

tmk:typedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "typed by"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:comment """The record that put this concept in one of the kinds.

Present so a typed concept can be traced to whoever or whatever typed it and the
date they did, rather than appearing to have always been a LegalTest. The kind
classes are the one place in this model where a class membership is a judgement
rather than a structural fact, so the judgement carries its provenance — and
since ADR-0079 that provenance is load-bearing rather than merely tidy, because
the typing may have been written by a model no expert has read.

Follow it and read what you find: the assertion node carries tmk:origin and
tmk:reviewStatus, and a concept whose only typing is authored has been sorted by
nobody."""@en-AU .

tmk:conceptGroup a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "concept group"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Assertion ;
    rdfs:comment """The group the typing record names, verbatim — including "none_of_these".

Redundant with the concept's class for the eleven kinds that have one, and the
only record of the residue. A concept typed none_of_these asserts no class on
purpose: none of the kinds fit it. Without this property that decision is
indistinguishable in the graph from a concept nobody has typed at all, and the
two are opposites — one is an answer about the taxonomy, the other is a
gap (ADR-0093)."""@en-AU .

tmk:LegalProposition a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Legal proposition"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A statement that something is so, held together with the passage that states
it and the authority of that passage.

Distinct from tmk:Assertion, which is this repo's record *that* a proposition
was extracted and approved. The proposition is what the corpus says; the
assertion is what this project did about it."""@en-AU .

# --- the scheme ------------------------------------------------------------

tmk:ConceptScheme a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf skos:ConceptScheme ;
    rdfs:label "TM-Knowledge concept scheme"@en-AU .

# --- properties on a concept ------------------------------------------------

tmk:notLabel a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "not label"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A term this concept must NOT be conflated with. There is no SKOS property for
this and it is the most valuable field on the record.

`deceptively similar` is a not-label of `likely to deceive or cause confusion`
because the two belong to different sections; a retrieval system that treats
them as synonyms routes an examiner to the wrong test, and the misrouting is
invisible because both destinations are about confusion (GX-0005)."""@en-AU .

tmk:definitionSource a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "definition source"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """The passages from which the concept's meaning is drawn.

Note what is NOT asserted alongside it: `skos:definition`. The approved records
carry definition *sources* and no definition text, so the graph carries sources
and no definition text. Writing one would be authoring the vocabulary
(CLAUDE.md rule 1); the absence is reported by `tmk-ontology-report`."""@en-AU .

tmk:legislativeBasis a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:range tmk:LegislativeProvision ;
    rdfs:label "legislative basis"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The provision the concept operates under. A concept can have a legislative basis and still be a creature of practice — the basis says which ground it belongs to, not that the Act uses the word."@en-AU .

tmk:goldRecord a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "gold record id"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The `eval/gold/` record this node was built from — `GC-0001`, `GR-0007`. The route back from any node in the graph to the approved record and the reviewer who signed it."@en-AU .

# --- the domain and range that licenses nothing false ----------------------

tmk:LegalMatter a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Legal matter"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """Anything an approved relation may hold between: a concept, a provision, a
Manual passage, a decision.

It exists because the approved relationships do not agree on narrower types.
`requiresElement` runs Provision→Concept twice, Concept→Concept three times and
Concept→Provision once; `excludesBasis` runs both ways. Asserting a narrow
`rdfs:domain` on that evidence would, under OWL RL, silently reclassify every
subject of the property — a careless range assertion manufactures
classifications (ontology/README.md).

So the asserted domain and range are this union superclass, which licenses only
'this is a legal matter', and the narrow types each predicate has actually been
seen with are recorded as annotations that infer nothing. Tightening them is an
expert ruling, and `tmk-ontology-report` lists the candidates."""@en-AU .

tmk:LegalConcept rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalMatter .
tmk:LegislativeProvision rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalMatter .
tmk:ManualInstruction rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalMatter .
tmk:Decision rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalMatter .
tmk:Chunk rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegalMatter .

tmk:ApprovedRelation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Approved relation"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A predicate on the closed list in `relations.ttl`.

Extraction may only propose relationships from this list. An open predicate set
makes precision unmeasurable — with no fixed vocabulary, a wrong predicate and a
new one are the same event, so nothing can be scored (ontology/README.md)."""@en-AU .

tmk:AdmittedRelation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Admitted relation"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A predicate on the closed list that no signed record uses yet — admitted by the owner's ruling of 2026-10-08 on the ontology review (C3, D6, A4; ADR-0123): the authority predicates that say who holds power over whom, and the structural isPartyTo and isProtectedThrough.

On the list on the same footing as tmk:ApprovedRelation for what a record may use. It differs only in that no expert has yet signed a relationship using it, so every use of it is machine-written and unreviewed."""@en-AU .

tmk:reading a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "reading"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "How a triple with this predicate is read aloud, subject first. A record whose triple does not read this way is recorded the wrong way round (review D1). Machine-written and unreviewed."@en-AU .

tmk:counterExample a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "counter-example"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "What this predicate is not for, with the predicate to use instead, and the record that made the mistake where one did. Machine-written and unreviewed."@en-AU .

tmk:admittedBy a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "admitted by"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Why the predicate is in the dictionary: `signed` (a signed record uses it), `ADR-0113` (SKOS), or `owner-2026-10-08` (the owner's ruling on the ontology review)."@en-AU .

tmk:predicateAuthority a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "predicate authority"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "`law` where the relation exists because legislation says so — a delegation, an appeal, a consent — and `practice` where it is office practice the Manual records, such as consulting or escalating. CLAUDE.md rule 5 keeps the two apart at every point, including on an edge. Absent on structural and vocabulary predicates."@en-AU .

tmk:authoredUsageCount a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "authored usage count"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "How many machine-written relationships use this predicate. Counted apart from tmk:usageCount and never added to it (ADR-0080 consequence 3)."@en-AU .

tmk:observedSubjectType a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "observed subject type"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A type this predicate's subject has actually taken, in either store, with no claim that it is the only one. An annotation, so it licenses no inference."@en-AU .

tmk:observedObjectType a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "observed object type"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "As tmk:observedSubjectType, for the object."@en-AU .

tmk:usageCount a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "usage count"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "How many signed relationships use this predicate. Several are used exactly once, which is worth seeing before anyone treats the dictionary as settled; tmk:authoredUsageCount counts the machine-written ones apart."@en-AU .

tmk:usedBy a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "used by"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The gold record ids that use this predicate. The route from a term in the dictionary back to the sentences that put it there."@en-AU .

# Comparison keys (review F2, ADR-0125). Computed by the graph build from each label
# with `ontology.hygiene.fold` — case, punctuation, a leading article and plurals
# folded — and shown to nobody. They let the SHACL gate join two concepts on a shared
# name without folding every label inside SPARQL.

tmk:labelKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "label key"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The folded form of one of the concept's names, preferred or alternative. Computed; a comparison key, not a label."@en-AU .

tmk:prefLabelKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf tmk:labelKey ;
    rdfs:label "preferred label key"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The folded form of the concept's preferred label. Computed; a comparison key, not a label."@en-AU .

tmk:notLabelKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:LegalConcept ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "not-label key"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The folded form of one of the concept's not-labels. Computed; a comparison key, not a label."@en-AU .
